build('E411','Morphology and Syntax','Morphology & Syntax','Morphology explains the structure of words; syntax explains how words form larger structures. Track meaningful units, grammatical categories and hierarchical relationships, then connect form with sound, meaning and clause organisation.',['Identify the pieces: lexemes, morphemes, bases and affixes.','Explain the patterns: formation, allomorphy and interfaces.','Build larger structures: categories, phrases, clauses and dependencies.','Compare analyses: structural rules and alternative syntactic theories.'],groups411,rows411,ess411,challenge411)
groups412=[('intro','Introduction to psycholinguistics',['Definition of psycholinguistics','History and development','Scope of psycholinguistics','Branches of psycholinguistics']),('child','Child language acquisition',['Pre-linguistic stage','Babbling stage','Sensori-motor stage','Holophrastic stage','Two-word stage','Telegraphic stage']),('l1','L1 acquisition theories',['Behaviourist theory','Mentalist theory','Biological theory','Cognitive theory','Sociological theory']),('l2','L2 learning theories',['Monitor Model','Interlanguage theory','Linguistic universals','Acculturation theory','Cognitive theory of L2 learning']),('individual','Individual factors in L2 learning',['Age','Aptitude','Attitude','Motivation','Personality','Cognitive style','Memory','Transfer of training','Intelligence','Fossilization']),('neuro','Neurolinguistics',['Aphasia','Dyslexia','Language therapy'])]
rows412='''
intro|Psycholinguistics|Definition of psycholinguistics;Scope of psycholinguistics|4,5,6,9,10|Psycholinguistics studies the mental knowledge and processes involved in acquiring, understanding and producing language.|It examines how linguistic representations interact with attention, memory and other cognitive processes; mental processes are inferred from evidence rather than directly read from behaviour.|A listener rapidly combines word meanings with sentence structure to understand a spoken question.|Knowing grammatical rules explicitly is different from using tacit linguistic knowledge.|Why can fluent speakers use a grammatical pattern without explaining its rule?|Much linguistic knowledge is tacit. Psycholinguistics distinguishes the representations that support use from a person's ability to state those representations consciously.
intro|History of psycholinguistics|History and development|6,7,8,9|Psycholinguistics developed through exchanges between psychology and linguistics, with a major mid-twentieth-century revival.|Early work examined language and thought; behaviourist approaches stressed learning histories, while cognitive and generative work renewed interest in internal representations.|The contrast between Skinner's account and Chomsky's critique helped shape debates about language learning.|The field did not begin with one person or a single experiment.|Why was the cognitive turn important for psycholinguistics?|It made internal representations and mental processes legitimate targets of explanation, encouraging experimental models of comprehension, production and acquisition beyond observable stimulus–response descriptions.
intro|Branches of psycholinguistics|Branches of psycholinguistics|11,12,13|Psycholinguistic branches include experimental, developmental and neurolinguistic approaches to language.|Experimental work studies processing through tasks; developmental work studies acquisition and change; neurolinguistics relates language to brain organisation.|A child-language recording and an adult reaction-time experiment investigate different dimensions of language processing.|The branches overlap rather than forming sealed disciplines.|How can one study be both developmental and experimental?|It can compare children at different stages using a controlled language task. Developmental describes the growth question, while experimental describes how the evidence is obtained.
intro|Speech production|Scope of psycholinguistics|12,13|Speech production transforms a communicative intention into a planned and articulated utterance.|A useful model distinguishes conceptualisation, formulation and articulation, with monitoring of the output. Real-time coordination is complex and partly automatic.|A speaker chooses a message, selects words and grammatical patterns, then produces their sounds.|An occasional speech error does not mean the speaker lacks the relevant grammar.|What can a slip of the tongue reveal about production?|A systematic slip can show that speakers plan and organise linguistic units before articulation. The specific error pattern supplies evidence about representations and timing.
intro|Comprehension and context|Scope of psycholinguistics|5,12|Comprehension builds an interpretation from linguistic input using knowledge and context.|Listeners and readers identify forms, assemble structure and integrate meanings. Bottom-up input and top-down expectations can interact.|The context of a river helps select the intended meaning of bank.|Context can guide interpretation but does not make the acoustic or written input irrelevant.|Why can the same word be recognised differently in different contexts?|Context activates different expectations and meanings, which interact with the incoming form. The listener must still connect that interpretation with the linguistic evidence.
child|Pre-linguistic development|Pre-linguistic stage|16,17,18|Pre-linguistic development includes early perception, vocalisation and communicative behaviour before stable words.|Infants attend to speech, participate in interaction and gradually tune perception to the languages around them. These abilities develop at varying rates.|A baby alternates gaze and vocalisation with a caregiver before producing recognisable words.|The absence of spoken words does not imply the absence of communicative learning.|Why is early turn-taking relevant before a child has words?|It provides experience of coordinated communication, attention and response. Language learning involves these interactional patterns as well as learning sound forms.
child|Babbling|Babbling stage|18,19|Babbling is early production of speech-like syllabic patterns before stable meaningful words.|Repeated and varied consonant–vowel sequences provide experience with articulation and interact with the ambient language. Babbling and later words show continuities and changes.|Repeated ba-ba or varied ba-da are possible babbling patterns.|A repeated sound becomes a word only when it has a sufficiently stable meaningful use.|Why is a baby's repeated mama not automatically its first word?|It may be a babbled sound sequence without a consistent referential or communicative meaning. Meaningful use in context is needed to identify a word.
child|Sensori-motor development|Sensori-motor stage|20,21|In Piaget's account, sensori-motor development concerns learning through perception and action during early life.|Object permanence, intentional action and symbolic capacities provide a cognitive context for language development. Cognitive stages should not be treated as exact universal language timetables.|A child searching for a hidden object shows an understanding relevant to representing absent things.|Sensori-motor stage is a cognitive-development label, not a specific grammatical stage.|How might object permanence support later language use?|Representing objects that are out of sight helps support reference beyond the immediate scene, though it does not by itself explain the acquisition of grammar.
child|Holophrastic stage|Holophrastic stage|21,22,23|At the holophrastic stage, a single word can perform a communicative act that adults express more fully.|Context, gesture and intonation help an adult interpret the intention; an adult paraphrase does not prove that the child represents a complete adult sentence internally.|Milk may function as a request for milk, depending on the setting.|One-word speech need not correspond to one fixed sentence meaning.|Why can the word more count as a complete communicative act in child speech?|In an interaction it can request continuation or an additional item. Context supplies information that an adult might express through a longer utterance.
child|Two-word combinations|Two-word stage|23,24|Two-word speech combines lexical items to express relationships such as agent–action or possession.|Early combinations show emerging organisation, often without the full set of adult function words and inflections.|Mummy go can link an agent with an action; daddy shoe can signal possession.|Two adjacent words are not necessarily evidence of complete adult-like syntax.|How can two-word speech reveal more than vocabulary growth?|The child combines words into meaningful relations and often uses language-appropriate ordering patterns, showing emerging structural knowledge rather than isolated naming alone.
child|Telegraphic speech|Telegraphic stage|24,25|Telegraphic speech uses short multiword combinations with many content words but fewer function words or inflections.|It can express increasingly varied meanings while grammatical detail continues to develop. The term describes a broad pattern, with substantial individual variation.|Mummy give ball communicates an event while omitting several adult grammatical elements.|Missing function words do not imply that the utterance lacks organisation.|Why is telegraphic speech not simply a random list of nouns and verbs?|Its words often follow meaningful ordering patterns and express relationships among participants and events. It is reduced relative to adult grammar, not necessarily unstructured.
child|Overgeneralisation and the Wug test|Telegraphic stage|26,27,28|Overgeneralisation applies a learned pattern beyond its conventional range; novel-word tasks test productive knowledge.|Children can use inflections on unfamiliar forms, supporting the idea that they generalise patterns rather than merely memorise all complete words.|A child who says goed applies a regular past-tense pattern to an irregular verb.|A developmental error can provide evidence of learning, not just failure.|What does the Wug test show about children's morphological knowledge?|Successful pluralisation of a novel noun indicates that children can generalise a morphological pattern beyond familiar memorised words. Performance also depends on task and developmental factors.
child|Caretaker speech|Pre-linguistic stage|28,29,30|Caretaker or child-directed speech is speech adapted to interaction with a child.|It may involve exaggerated prosody, repetition, shorter utterances and responsive interaction, but its forms vary across cultures and contexts.|A caregiver repeats a label while following the child's attention to an object.|One culturally familiar style of baby talk is not a universal prerequisite for acquisition.|Why should child-directed speech not be defined as universally ungrammatical baby talk?|Much child-directed speech is grammatical, and caregiver styles vary. Its relevant properties include attention, responsiveness and accessibility rather than one obligatory simplified code.
child|Critical and sensitive periods|Pre-linguistic stage|31,32,33|A critical-period hypothesis proposes age-related constraints on language acquisition; sensitive-period accounts allow more gradual changes.|Different language abilities may show different age patterns. Age-related outcomes interact with experience and learning opportunities, so a single rigid cutoff is too simple.|Early and late learners may differ in ultimate pronunciation even when older beginners initially progress quickly.|A decline in average attainment does not mean adults cannot learn a language successfully.|Why does evidence for an age effect not establish one absolute deadline for all language learning?|Age patterns can differ by skill, task and experience, and observational findings require interpretation. A sensitive period can involve gradual changes rather than total loss of learning ability.
child|Deprivation evidence|Pre-linguistic stage|32,33|Language-deprivation cases inform age questions but contain major confounds.|Severe isolation often involves trauma, poor care and uncertain developmental histories. Such cases cannot cleanly isolate age from every other influence.|A late first-language learner's difficulties may reflect both timing and broader deprivation.|One unusual case cannot by itself prove a universal biological cutoff.|Why is the case of Genie not a controlled experiment on language timing?|Language deprivation was accompanied by severe abuse and other developmental risks. These factors prevent a simple causal inference that age alone explains the outcome.
l1|Behaviourist acquisition theory|Behaviourist theory|35,36,37|Behaviourist accounts explain language learning through environmental associations, imitation and reinforcement.|Skinner's operant account emphasises consequences that shape verbal behaviour. It highlights input and learning history, while simple imitation struggles to explain productive novelty.|A caregiver's response can reinforce a useful request, yet the child also creates unfamiliar utterances.|Negative reinforcement removes an aversive condition; it is not the same as punishment.|Why do forms such as goed challenge a pure imitation account?|Children normally hear went, not goed. Such errors indicate generalisation beyond remembered adult forms, requiring an account of how productive patterns emerge.
l1|Mentalist and innatist theory|Mentalist theory|37,38,39|Mentalist or innatist approaches propose that language learning depends partly on inborn linguistic capacities.|Chomskyan accounts connect acquisition with Universal Grammar and the problem of explaining abstract knowledge from limited evidence. Particular formulations and empirical claims remain debated.|Children learn structural dependencies that are not taught as explicit rules.|Innateness does not mean learning occurs without linguistic experience.|What does poverty of the stimulus argue, and what is debated?|It argues that some acquired knowledge exceeds what input alone straightforwardly explains, motivating innate constraints. Researchers debate the input evidence, learning mechanisms and the nature of those constraints.
l1|Biological theory|Biological theory|39,40,41|Biological accounts relate language acquisition to species-specific capacities, maturation and brain development.|Lenneberg connected language development with biological timing. Modern interpretation requires attention to plasticity, distributed networks and continuing learning rather than an all-or-nothing developmental switch.|Children's capacity to acquire accessible spoken or signed language shows biological preparedness interacting with experience.|Biological contribution does not exclude social or cognitive explanation.|How can a biological capacity and environmental learning both be necessary?|A capacity provides the ability to develop language, while accessible interaction and input shape which language and patterns are acquired. Neither alone fully specifies an individual's development.
l1|Cognitive acquisition theory|Cognitive theory|41,42|Cognitive approaches connect language development with general processes such as categorisation, memory and pattern learning.|Piagetian accounts emphasise links with conceptual development; other cognitive learning views focus on meaningful learning and information processing.|A learner connects a new word to an existing concept and uses recurring patterns to extend its use.|Cognitive approaches are a family of explanations, not one identical theory.|Why might meaningful learning help retain vocabulary better than isolated repetition alone?|Connecting a word to concepts and experiences supplies richer retrieval relations. Repetition can still contribute, but its effects depend on how it is organised and used.
l1|Social interactionist theory|Sociological theory|43,44|Social interactionist accounts explain acquisition through communication, joint activity and responsive support.|Vygotskian approaches emphasise mediation and development through interaction; usage-based perspectives also examine how shared attention and recurring constructions support learning.|A caregiver expands a child's request while maintaining the shared activity.|Interaction is more than the physical presence of another speaker.|Why is joint attention useful in learning a new word?|It helps establish what participants are referring to and doing together, narrowing the possible interpretation of the label within a meaningful interaction.
l1|Nature and nurture|Behaviourist theory;Mentalist theory;Biological theory;Cognitive theory;Sociological theory|35,45,46|The nature–nurture debate concerns how biological preparedness and experience contribute to language development.|Acquisition theories differ in what they treat as built in and which mechanisms they assign to learning. A useful comparison identifies the evidence each explains and its limits.|Input supplies language-specific patterns, while human learners bring cognitive and biological capacities to that input.|Showing that input matters does not settle exactly what is innate.|Why is simply choosing nature or nurture a weak answer about acquisition?|Language development involves both capacities and experience. The theoretical question concerns their specific contributions and mechanisms, not whether only one exists.
l2|Krashen's Monitor Model|Monitor Model|47,48,49|Krashen's Monitor Model proposes five hypotheses concerning acquisition, learning, monitoring, natural order, input and affect.|The model distinguishes acquired competence from conscious learning and stresses comprehensible input. Its strong separation of learning and acquisition and the testability of its concepts have been criticised.|A learner may know a rule explicitly yet fail to use it spontaneously in rapid conversation.|Knowing a rule and using it fluently are different facts; that distinction alone does not prove the model's full claims.|Name the five hypotheses in Krashen's model.|The acquisition–learning distinction, Monitor hypothesis, natural order hypothesis, Input hypothesis and Affective Filter hypothesis.
l2|Acquisition, learning and monitoring|Monitor Model|47,48,49|In Krashen's account, acquisition is subconscious development and learning is conscious rule knowledge used for monitoring.|Monitoring is proposed to require time, attention to form and knowledge of a rule. These conditions constrain its usefulness during spontaneous communication.|A learner checks subject–verb agreement while editing a written sentence.|The claim that conscious knowledge cannot become acquired competence is theoretically disputed.|Why might a learner edit writing accurately but make similar errors when speaking quickly?|Editing permits time and attention to form, while rapid production draws on readily available processing. This difference is compatible with monitoring but does not uniquely confirm Krashen's whole model.
l2|Comprehensible input and affect|Monitor Model|48,49|Krashen proposes that acquisition is supported by comprehensible input beyond the learner's current level and facilitated by lower affective barriers.|The notation i+1 is a conceptual description, not a simple measurable syllabus step. Input is important, while output, interaction and attention are also relevant in wider SLA research.|A comprehensible story introduces a few unfamiliar forms in a meaningful context.|Comprehensibility does not mean a text contains no new language.|What is a limitation of using i+1 as an exact classroom formula?|The learner's i and the next step are difficult to specify precisely. Teachers can provide meaningful, accessible challenge, but the formula does not mechanically determine individual learning.
l2|Interlanguage|Interlanguage theory|49,50,51,52|Interlanguage is a learner's developing linguistic system, with patterns distinct from both the first language and target norms.|It can be systematic, changing and variable across contexts. Selinker related its development to transfer, training, strategies, communication and overgeneralisation.|A learner consistently omits third-person -s but changes usage as experience and context change.|Learner language is more than a random collection of mistakes.|Why does a repeated learner error sometimes support an interlanguage analysis?|A regular pattern suggests a developing rule or representation. Analyse where the form occurs and how it changes rather than treating each instance as unrelated failure.
l2|Linguistic universals|Linguistic universals|52,53,54|Linguistic universals are proposed cross-linguistic properties or constraints, studied through typology and theories such as Universal Grammar.|Typological approaches examine language samples and implicational patterns; generative approaches investigate constraints on possible grammars. L2 access to UG is a debated issue.|A typological implication predicts that one structural property is associated with another across languages.|A frequent cross-linguistic tendency is not automatically an exceptionless universal.|How do typological universals and Universal Grammar differ as approaches?|Typology infers patterns from comparisons of languages. Universal Grammar proposes constraints or principles of the language faculty; their explanations and methods differ.
l2|Acculturation theory|Acculturation theory|54,55,56|Schumann's acculturation account links aspects of second-language development to social and psychological relations with the target-language community.|Social distance, attitudes and opportunities for contact can constrain learning. The theory offers a contextual perspective but does not fully explain cognitive mechanisms or every classroom setting.|A learner may have extensive residence but limited access to interaction with target-language users.|Residence length does not automatically measure meaningful contact or social inclusion.|Why might years in an L2 environment fail to predict high proficiency?|Access, social distance, identity, work patterns and interaction opportunities differ. Time alone does not reveal the quality or range of language experience.
l2|McLaughlin's cognitive theory|Cognitive theory of L2 learning|56,57|McLaughlin treats L2 learning as complex skill development involving controlled and increasingly automatic processing.|Practice can reorganise knowledge and processing; restructuring can produce temporary performance changes. Attention is limited, so fluency involves efficient access rather than simply more explicit rules.|An initially effortful pattern becomes easier to retrieve in a familiar communicative task.|Automaticity is not identical to perfect accuracy or absence of meaning.|How can a temporary performance decline occur during learning?|Restructuring can change an earlier system and temporarily disrupt performance. A brief decline need not mean that no learning is taking place.
l2|Sociocultural theory and ZPD|Cognitive theory of L2 learning|58,59|Sociocultural theory explains development through mediation and participation in social activity.|The zone of proximal development concerns what learners can achieve with appropriate support beyond independent performance. Scaffolding should respond to need and be adjusted over time.|A partner's prompt helps a learner complete an explanation they cannot yet produce alone.|ZPD is not simply every task that is difficult.|What distinguishes scaffolding from giving a learner the full answer?|Scaffolding supports the learner's own activity with contingent prompts or tools and can be reduced as independence grows. Supplying the answer may leave the learner's processing unchanged.
individual|Age and ultimate attainment|Age|63,64|Age can relate differently to initial learning rate and ultimate attainment.|Older beginners may progress quickly in some instructed tasks, while earlier sustained exposure can favour later attainment in some domains. Experience, skill and context shape the pattern.|An adult may learn classroom vocabulary faster initially while a child develops more target-like pronunciation over time.|Younger is not automatically better on every measure at every stage.|Why distinguish rate from ultimate attainment when comparing child and adult learners?|A group can learn faster initially yet reach a different long-term outcome. A short course and many years of exposure answer different age-related questions.
individual|Language aptitude|Aptitude|64,65|Language aptitude refers to abilities that help explain differences in the ease or rate of language learning.|Classic components include phonetic coding, grammatical sensitivity, inductive learning and associative memory. Aptitude interacts with tasks and instruction.|One learner quickly detects an unfamiliar grammatical pattern; another excels at remembering sound–word associations.|Aptitude is not a total measure of a person's worth or a fixed guarantee of success.|How does aptitude differ from motivation?|Aptitude concerns abilities relevant to learning; motivation concerns goals, effort and persistence. They can interact, but they are different constructs.
individual|Attitude|Attitude|65,66|Attitude is an evaluative orientation toward a language, its users or the learning situation.|Attitudes can influence engagement and be reshaped by experience and social context. Associations with success need careful causal interpretation.|A learner's positive view of a classroom community may encourage participation.|An attitude is not identical to proficiency or an observable amount of effort.|Can successful learning change attitude as well as attitude influencing learning?|Yes. The relationship can be reciprocal: experience and achievement may strengthen positive attitudes, while attitudes can affect engagement.
individual|Motivation|Motivation|67,68|Motivation concerns the direction, intensity and persistence of effort toward language-learning goals.|Instrumental and integrative orientations concern reasons for learning; intrinsic and extrinsic distinctions concern the source or character of engagement. Motivation changes over time.|A learner studies for employment and also enjoys communicating in the language.|Instrumental reasons do not automatically produce weak learning.|Can instrumental and intrinsic motivation coexist?|Yes. A learner may want a qualification and also enjoy the activity itself. Motivational orientations can overlap and change with experience.
individual|Personality, anxiety and willingness to communicate|Personality|68,69,70|Personality-related factors and anxiety can affect participation and learning opportunities, with effects depending on task and setting.|Extraversion may support some interaction, while introverted learners may succeed in other tasks. Willingness to communicate changes by topic, partner and perceived safety.|A learner speaks freely with a friend but stays silent before an examiner.|A quiet learner is not necessarily unmotivated or less capable.|Why should an examiner-facing silence not be treated as proof of poor language knowledge?|Anxiety, processing pressure and the social setting can limit access or willingness to speak. Performance reflects more than underlying knowledge alone.
individual|Cognitive style and learning-style claims|Cognitive style|70,71|Cognitive style describes habitual approaches to processing information; preferences do not establish that matching instruction to a style improves learning.|Field dependence and independence are classic constructs in the guide. Measures overlap with other abilities, and style categories should not be treated as fixed prescriptions.|A learner may prefer diagrams while still benefiting from explanations suited to the subject matter.|Preference and demonstrated instructional effectiveness are different kinds of evidence.|What evidence would support teaching each student only through a preferred learning style?|It would require a credible interaction showing that learners with different assessed styles benefit from different matched methods. Preference reports alone do not establish that benefit.
individual|Memory and working memory|Memory|71,72|Memory supports storage and retrieval of language; working memory maintains and manipulates information during ongoing activity.|Phonological memory can support unfamiliar word forms, while meaningful connections and retrieval affect longer-term access. Capacity is task-dependent rather than a single explanation of success.|A learner holds earlier words in mind while interpreting the end of a long sentence.|Remembering a rule explicitly is not the same as accessing it efficiently in conversation.|Why can a long sentence be difficult despite containing familiar words?|Maintaining and integrating its parts places processing demands on working memory and structural interpretation. Vocabulary familiarity alone does not remove those demands.
individual|Transfer and transfer of training|Transfer of training|72,73|Transfer uses prior knowledge in new learning; transfer of training concerns influences of previous teaching or practice.|Similarity can support performance, while differences or learned routines can encourage non-target patterns. Not every learner error originates in L1.|A repeatedly drilled answer format may be overused in conversation even where it is unnatural.|L1 transfer and transfer of training have different sources.|A learner answers every casual question with a full rehearsed sentence. What may explain it?|Transfer of training may be involved if classroom routines strongly reinforced that response pattern. Examine teaching history rather than automatically attributing it to the first language.
individual|Intelligence and language learning|Intelligence|73,74|Intelligence measures can relate to some language-learning tasks, especially academic and analytical ones, without explaining all communicative development.|Different abilities and opportunities contribute to outcomes. Multiple-intelligences proposals should not be treated as proof that matching fixed learner types improves instruction.|An analytic test score may relate to grammar study more directly than to comfortable conversation.|A low score on one intelligence test does not imply inability to acquire or use language.|Why does an IQ score fail to provide a complete prediction of conversational proficiency?|Conversation also involves exposure, interaction, motivation, contextual support and other abilities. A general score cannot represent every process or opportunity involved.
individual|Fossilization and stabilisation|Fossilization|75,76|Fossilization describes persistent stabilisation of interlanguage features despite opportunities for further development.|Permanence is difficult to establish; a long-lasting pattern may change with new conditions. Distinguish a short plateau from stronger claims of fossilization.|A learner persistently omits an inflection over many contexts and years.|One repeated error or brief learning plateau is insufficient evidence of permanent cessation.|Why is fossilization difficult to prove empirically?|It requires a strong claim about persistence, yet researchers cannot observe all future development. Longitudinal evidence can document stabilisation without guaranteeing permanent impossibility of change.
individual|Identity, investment and learner strategies|Motivation;Personality|76,77|Learner identity and access to valued activities shape how motivation can be put into practice.|Investment relates participation to social identities and resources. Strategies include deliberate ways of organising learning or communication, whose effectiveness depends on task and context.|An interested learner may speak little because peers exclude their contributions.|Low participation is not always an individual motivational failure.|How can a learner be motivated but have little chance to improve through interaction?|Social exclusion or unequal access can restrict participation despite strong desire. Learning opportunities depend partly on relationships and institutions, not only individual effort.
neuro|Neurolinguistics and lateralisation|Aphasia|80,81,82|Neurolinguistics investigates relationships between language processes and brain organisation.|Language is often left-lateralised, but multiple regions and pathways contribute; right-hemisphere processes can support prosody and discourse. Individual patterns vary.|A language task can involve a network rather than one isolated point in the brain.|Lateralisation does not mean one hemisphere performs all language alone.|Why is left-lateralised language not equivalent to saying the right hemisphere has no role?|Lateralisation describes relative specialisation, not exclusive control. Language activity involves interacting systems, including functions often associated with the right hemisphere.
neuro|Aphasia|Aphasia|83,84|Aphasia is an acquired language disorder associated with brain damage that can affect speaking, comprehension, reading or writing.|Profiles vary with the affected network and individual history. Language impairment should not be equated with global loss of intelligence.|A person may struggle to retrieve words after a stroke while retaining many other abilities.|Aphasia is not simply a disorder of the speech muscles.|How can aphasia affect a person who can still move the speech organs?|The impairment concerns language representations or processing, such as word retrieval or comprehension, rather than only the motor ability to produce sound.
neuro|Broca-type and Wernicke-type profiles|Aphasia|83,84|Classic Broca-type aphasia often involves effortful nonfluent production; Wernicke-type profiles often involve fluent but impaired meaningful communication and comprehension.|These are useful historical clinical patterns, not perfectly separate language boxes. Symptoms and lesion locations do not map one to one in every individual.|An effortful short utterance and a fluent jargon-filled utterance can reflect contrasting language difficulties.|Fluent speech does not guarantee intact comprehension, and nonfluency does not guarantee fully intact comprehension.|Why should a fluent aphasic utterance not be taken as evidence of intact language?|Fluency concerns flow. Word selection, meaningfulness and comprehension may still be impaired, so several language components must be assessed.
neuro|Dyslexia|Dyslexia|84,85|Dyslexia involves persistent difficulty with accurate or fluent word reading and related literacy processes.|Developmental and acquired reading difficulties should be distinguished. Phonological processing often matters, while profiles vary; intelligence alone does not define reading skill.|A learner may understand spoken explanations well but struggle to decode unfamiliar written words.|Dyslexia is not adequately explained as laziness or simply seeing letters backwards.|Why can strong oral understanding coexist with reading difficulty?|Word recognition and decoding place demands that differ from understanding accessible spoken language. Uneven development shows that these abilities should be considered separately.
neuro|Language therapy|Language therapy|86|Language therapy aims to support communication by restoring skills where possible and developing compensatory strategies.|Intervention follows assessment of the individual's needs and goals and may involve family, educational or clinical support. Outcomes vary and improvement can continue beyond an early recovery phase.|Practising word retrieval and using communication aids can address different functional needs.|Language therapy is not one universal drill for every language difficulty.|Why should therapy goals be individualised rather than based only on a diagnostic label?|People with the same broad label can have different strengths, difficulties and communication needs. Assessment guides which tasks or compensatory tools are appropriate.
neuro|Brain imaging and behavioural methods|Aphasia|87,88|Neurolinguistic research combines behavioural evidence with methods that examine brain structure or activity.|MRI can show anatomy; functional methods examine task-related signals; EEG and event-related potentials provide fine temporal information. Each method has limits of inference.|A behavioural task and an ERP measure can address different aspects of a meaning-processing question.|Activation does not by itself prove that a region is exclusively responsible for a function.|Why combine reaction-time evidence with a brain measure?|They provide complementary observations about performance and neural processes. Converging evidence can constrain an explanation more effectively than interpreting either measure alone.
neuro|Plasticity and modularity|Aphasia;Language therapy|88,89,90,91|Plasticity concerns change in brain organisation; modularity concerns the degree to which language processes are specialised.|Recovery and developmental adaptation show flexibility, while dissociations suggest partially distinguishable abilities. Neither observation requires completely isolated, fixed modules.|Different patterns of impairment can help distinguish word retrieval from other cognitive abilities.|A dissociation supports a distinction but does not automatically prove total independence.|Why can a dissociation between language and another ability be informative without proving an isolated language organ?|Different performance patterns suggest separable components or demands. The tasks may still depend on interacting networks, so stronger anatomical and theoretical claims require additional evidence.
'''
